---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import ExactRoundtrip from '../components/bytelite/ExactRoundtrip.astro';
import CurrentVsFinal from '../components/bytelite/CurrentVsFinal.astro';
import ByteAccounting from '../components/bytelite/ByteAccounting.astro';
import ReductionGate from '../components/bytelite/ReductionGate.astro';
import WhatItIsNot from '../components/bytelite/WhatItIsNot.astro';
import InfrastructureImpact from '../components/bytelite/InfrastructureImpact.astro';
import { DETERMINISM_STATEMENT, TARGET_STATEMENT, DEVELOPMENT_STATEMENT } from '../data/bytelite';
---

<Layout
  title="How ByteLite Works | ByteLite"
  description="The public principles behind ByteLite: determinism, exact reconstruction, a self-contained final artifact, complete counting, recursive processing and proof before claim. Explained with diagrams, not mechanism."
  keywords="how ByteLite works, deterministic lossless representation, exact reconstruction, self-contained artifact, complete accounting"
  canonical="https://www.thebytelite.com/how-it-works/"
>
  <Header slot="header" />

  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">How it works</p>
      <h1>Principles, not mechanism.</h1>
      <p class="hero-sub">
        This page explains what ByteLite must achieve and how it holds itself to account. The
        methods that produce a representation are proprietary and are not published here.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Public Review Framework.
       This is NOT the ByteLite algorithm, the processing pipeline, the encoding flow or the
       internal architecture, and it must never be captioned as any of those. It is the list of
       things an outside reviewer is allowed to examine. Naming it correctly is the whole point:
       a diagram captioned "how it works" would be an implementation disclosure. -->
  <section class="section-sm">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <h2 class="prf-h">What can be reviewed from outside</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        People ask what they are allowed to look at. This is the answer: six categories that can be
        examined without me handing over the mechanism. It is a review framework, not a description
        of how ByteLite operates internally.
      </p>
      <img
        class="fig"
        src="/bytelite/deterministic-flow-diagram.png"
        alt="Public review framework: source, result, evidence, status, limitations and review."
        width="1672" height="941" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
      />
      <ul class="prf-list">
        <li><strong>Source</strong> &mdash; the declared inputs.</li>
        <li><strong>Result</strong> &mdash; the observable output.</li>
        <li><strong>Evidence</strong> &mdash; the supporting artifacts.</li>
        <li><strong>Status</strong> &mdash; the current state, labelled.</li>
        <li><strong>Limitations</strong> &mdash; the boundaries I already know about.</li>
        <li><strong>Review</strong> &mdash; technical examination of all of the above.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 1. DETERMINISM -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <p class="step-num">01</p>
      <h2>Determinism</h2>
      <p class="lede">The same input and build produce the same output, every time.</p>
      <p class="body">{DETERMINISM_STATEMENT}</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 2. EXACT RECONSTRUCTION -->
  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <p class="step-num">02</p>
      <h2>Exact reconstruction</h2>
      <p class="lede">The original must come back byte for byte, or the attempt failed.</p>
      <ExactRoundtrip />
      <p class="body">
        This is the precondition for everything else. A representation that loses even one byte is
        not a smaller file &mdash; it is a different file.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 3. SELF-CONTAINED FINAL TARGET -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <p class="step-num">03</p>
      <h2>A self-contained final artifact</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        Everything the decoder needs is intended to live inside the artifact itself &mdash; not in
        a companion file that travels beside it.
      </p>
      <CurrentVsFinal />
      <p class="body">
        This is the clearest difference between where ByteLite is today and where it is going.
        Development deliberately writes reconstruction evidence out in the open so the architecture
        can be inspected, replayed and falsified. That scaffold is a research tool. The final
        artifact must stand alone.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 4. COMPLETE COUNTING -->
  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <p class="step-num">04</p>
      <h2>Complete counting</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        A reduction counts only when the complete artifact is smaller &mdash; everything included.
      </p>
      <ByteAccounting />
      <p class="body">
        This is the discipline that separates a measurement from a claim. If part of what the
        decoder needs lives somewhere that was not counted, the number is not real.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 5. THE CLAIM GATE -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <p class="step-num">05</p>
      <h2>Proof before claim</h2>
      <p class="lede">Two questions decide whether ByteLite may claim anything at all.</p>
      <ReductionGate />
      <p class="body">
        <strong class="target-note">Target:</strong> {TARGET_STATEMENT}
      </p>
      <p class="body">
        <strong class="dev-note">Current state:</strong> {DEVELOPMENT_STATEMENT} The gate above
        governs what ByteLite is willing to claim today; it is not a statement about how far the
        architecture is meant to reach.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 6. THE BOUNDARY -->
  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <p class="step-num">06</p>
      <h2>What ByteLite is not</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        The boundary, stated as refusals. None of these cards says what is used instead &mdash;
        that is the part that stays private.
      </p>
      <WhatItIsNot />

      <!-- Differentiator. "ByteLite Priorities" is the required wording and stays: these are
           design priorities, not benchmarked, certified or independently validated outcomes. -->
      <h3 class="diff-h">Different by design</h3>
      <p class="body">
        The column on the right is a list of priorities I chose, not a list of results anyone has
        certified. It says what ByteLite is built to be, which is a different claim from what it
        has been proven to do.
      </p>
      <img
        class="fig"
        src="/bytelite/byte-vs-blackbox-comparison.png"
        alt="A common probabilistic approach compared with ByteLite priorities: deterministic operation, explicit constraints, evidence tracked, auditable artifacts."
        width="1672" height="941" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
      />
      <ul class="prf-list">
        <li><strong>Deterministic operation</strong> instead of probabilistic outputs.</li>
        <li><strong>Explicit constraints</strong> instead of confidence scores.</li>
        <li><strong>Evidence tracked</strong> instead of limited explainability.</li>
        <li><strong>Auditable artifacts</strong> instead of statistical behaviour.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- TECHNICAL DETAIL, optional and collapsed -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <h2>Technical details</h2>
      <details class="drawer">
        <summary>Recursive processing, at a high level</summary>
        <p>
          The complete representation ByteLite produces is itself a candidate for further
          reduction. Whether another pass is taken is decided by the same accounting rule as the
          first: it counts only if the complete artifact gets smaller. A pass that does not improve
          the complete artifact is not taken.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details class="drawer">
        <summary>Intrinsic convergence, as a target</summary>
        <p>
          The intended behavior is that recursion stops because the work is finished, not because
          a counter ran out. No iteration cap, timeout or "good enough" threshold decides the
          outcome. Demonstrating that convergence is an open gate, not a completed result.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details class="drawer">
        <summary>Verification</summary>
        <p>
          Every result is checked by rebuilding the original and comparing it against the input.
          The size reported is counted from the finished artifact rather than estimated from a
          model of it.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details class="drawer">
        <summary>What is not published</summary>
        <p>
          The internal rules and formats that produce a representation are trade secrets and are
          not disclosed on this site, and neither is the way required reconstruction information is
          intended to become compact. The public material describes what ByteLite must prove, not
          how it is built.
        </p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- WHY IT COULD MATTER -->
  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">If it works</p>
      <h2>Why a smaller representation can matter.</h2>
      <InfrastructureImpact />
      <div class="cta-row">
        <a href="/validation" class="btn btn-primary">What has been proven</a>
        <a href="/licensing" class="btn btn-outline">Licensing</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Footer slot="footer" />
</Layout>

<style>
.narrow { max-width: 780px; }
.page-hero { padding: 4rem 0 2.5rem; }
.fig {
  display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 1.5rem 0;
  border: 1px solid #1a2235; border-radius: .75rem; background: #0d1120;
}
.prf-h { margin-bottom: 1rem; }
.diff-h { font-size: 1.125rem; color: #e4e7f2; margin: 2.5rem 0 .75rem; }
.prf-list { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; display: grid; gap: .5rem; }
.prf-list li {
  font-size: .9375rem; color: #a0a8c0; line-height: 1.65;
  padding: .625rem .875rem; background: #0d1120;
  border: 1px solid #1a2235; border-left: 3px solid #6366f1; border-radius: .5rem;
}
.prf-list strong { color: #e4e7f2; font-weight: 600; }
.hero-sub { max-width: 640px; font-size: 1.0625rem; margin-top: 1rem; color: #a0a8c0; line-height: 1.75; }
.step-num {
  font-family: ui-monospace, 'Courier New', monospace;
  font-size: .75rem; font-weight: 700; color: #6366f1; letter-spacing: .1em; margin-bottom: .5rem;
}
h2 { margin-bottom: .75rem; }
.lede { font-size: 1.0625rem; color: #c4c8f0; line-height: 1.75; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; }
.body { font-size: 1rem; color: #a0a8c0; line-height: 1.8; margin-top: 1.25rem; }
.target-note { color: #818cf8; }
.dev-note { color: #fbbf24; }
.cta-row { display: flex; gap: .75rem; flex-wrap: wrap; margin-top: 1.75rem; }

.drawer { border: 1px solid #1a2235; border-radius: .625rem; background: #0d1120; margin-bottom: .75rem; }
.drawer summary {
  cursor: pointer; padding: .875rem 1.125rem;
  font-size: .9375rem; font-weight: 600; color: #c4c8f0; list-style: none;
}
.drawer summary::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }
.drawer summary::before { content: "+ "; color: #818cf8; font-weight: 700; }
.drawer[open] summary::before { content: "\2212 "; }
.drawer summary:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid #6366f1; outline-offset: 2px; border-radius: .625rem; }
.drawer p { padding: 0 1.125rem 1.125rem; font-size: .9375rem; color: #a0a8c0; line-height: 1.75; margin: 0; }
</style>
